1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
It's tempting to picture the model writing out a whole answer in its head and then reading it to you.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
That's not what happens, and the difference actually matters for how you prompt a model.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,000
This lesson maps how the model really builds a response, one token at a time.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Three terms anchor this lesson. Next token generation is the mechanism by which the model produces

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:23,000
text, one token at a time,

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:26,000
continuing until it hits a stopping point.

7
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Autoregressive describes how it does that, generating left to right, where each token it produces

8
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:37,000
feeds right back in as input for choosing the next one.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:44,000
And conditioning is the idea that every token is chosen based on all the tokens before it, including

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
the model's own earlier outputs, not just your prompt.

11
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Put simply, the model writes forward, it commits to a token, adds it to the running text, and then

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,000
asks itself what comes next.

13
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,000
Over and over until it's done.

14
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Let me kill a common mental image because it leads people astray.

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
The model doesn't plan a complete answer somewhere inside and then reveal it.

16
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
There's no finished response sitting there waiting.

17
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:18,000
What actually happens is that it emits one token, then reconsiders the entire sequence so far to pick the next one.

18
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:22,000
Each token is conditioned on all the prior tokens.

19
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:28,000
So the response is genuinely built forward, token by token, with nothing pre-written behind the curtain.

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Why does this matter to you as a builder?

21
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Because it means the model can't fix an early commitment by referring to a plan it never made.

22
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Whatever it generates first becomes the ground it reasons from.

23
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Let's contrast the two mental models head to head, because picking the right one changes how you prompt.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
The wrong picture is compose, then emit.

25
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:57,000
In that version, the model writes a full answer internally, checks it over, and hands you the finished thing.

26
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
It's intuitive, and it's just not how these models work.

27
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
The right picture is generate, left to right.

28
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,000
The model produces each token in sequence, with no look-ahead to a completed draft it's working toward.

29
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:14,000
Two pictures, and only the second one is accurate.

30
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Once you internalize that the model is always writing forward with no safety net of a pre-planned

31
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,000
answer, a lot of prompting advice suddenly makes sense.

32
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Here's the practical payoff of left-to-right generation.

33
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Because each token conditions the ones after it, the opening of a response has outsized influence.

34
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
A strong format anchor early, say, the first line of a JSON object, or a clear heading, tends to

35
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:45,000
keep the whole output on that track.

36
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:46,000
The flip side is the risk.

37
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
If the first few tokens drift off in the wrong direction, that drift compounds as the model keeps

38
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,000
conditioning on its own wandering output.

39
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:59,000
This is why structured openings make parsing so much more reliable.

40
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:02,000
You're not just being tidy, you're steering the generation.

41
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
Set the pattern early, and the model will usually hold it all the way through.

42
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:11,000
This left-to-right nature is also what makes streaming possible.

43
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Since the model generates tokens one at a time, you don't have to wait for the whole response to be finished.

44
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Servers send events surface each token as it's produced, so your interface can start showing text almost immediately.

45
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:32,000
The big win is perceived speed, a faster first byte, because the user sees progress instead of a spinner.

46
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Here's the key point, though.

47
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Streaming only changes how the output is delivered to you.

48
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:41,000
It doesn't change how the tokens are produced under the hood.

49
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:45,000
The generation is exactly the same, autoregressive and forward.

50
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Streaming just hands you the pieces as they come, rather than all at once.

51
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Let's turn this into prompting practice, because you can actively exploit it.

52
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Since generation runs left-to-right and early, tokens steer the rest, lead with the format you want

53
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:03,000
the answer to follow.

54
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:10,000
If you need a specific structure, show it up front and give the model a clear first line to continue from.

55
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:14,000
That anchors the pattern before it has a chance to wander.

56
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:23,000
And when an exact shape really matters, prefer structured outputs over hoping the model lands the format on its own.

57
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:25,000
The theme is the same throughout.

58
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Steer the start, get the opening tokens pointed the right way, and the rest of the response usually

59
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,000
falls in line behind them.

60
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Let's tie the three lessons together, because they're really one picture.

61
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Tokens are the unit.

62
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:45,000
They're generated one at a time, and they're the same unit that fills your context window and shows up on your bill.

63
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Sampling is how each next token gets picked from the distribution, and that process is

64
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:54,000
probabilistic, which is exactly why output is non-deterministic.

65
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
And generation is the left-to-right loop that turns all those individual picks into a full response.

66
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Tokens, sampling, generation.

67
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
One reads in and out as budget, one chooses, one assembles.

68
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
When you hold all three at once, the model stops being a black box and starts being a system you can design around.

69
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:18,000
So what does all of this mean for you when you sit down to build?

70
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:20,000
Three habits fall out of it.

71
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
First, budget your tokens deliberately.

72
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Everything shares the context window, so plan what goes in and prune what doesn't need to be there.

73
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,000
Second, expect variation.

74
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Output is non-deterministic, so validate every response instead of trusting an exact string.

75
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:41,000
Third, anchor your format early.

76
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:46,000
Because generation goes left to right, the opening tokens steer the rest.

77
00:05:46,000 --> 00:05:48,000
So lead with the structure you want.

78
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:55,000
Design for how the model actually works, token by token, and your systems get more predictable, not

79
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,000
less, even though the model itself never will be perfectly repeatable.

80
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:02,000
Let's frame the big picture.

81
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:05,000
The model builds every response one token at a time.

82
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:07,000
What to look for?

83
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:14,000
Generation is autoregressive and left to right, and each token conditions on all prior tokens.

84
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:17,000
The trap is expecting a pre-planned answer.

85
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:21,000
And remember, streaming just delivers tokens as they're produced.

86
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:26,000
And that covers the next token generation model, one token at a time.

87
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,000
When you're ready, I'll see you in the next one.

